course: dictionary
lesson: 9617
title: Strontium: the red metal in fireworks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Strontium is a chemical element, a pure substance made of only one kind of atom. Its short name is Sr, and its atomic number is 38.
explain: Strontium is a soft, silvery metal. «The colours of fireworks come from different metals: strontium gives red, barium green, sodium yellow and copper blue.» The sentence tells you that strontium is the metal that makes fireworks red.
explain: Every metal makes its own colour in a firework. «Every kind of metal gives back its own colour: strontium red, barium green, sodium yellow, copper blue.» If you swap the colour for another metal's colour, the sentence stops making sense, so you know each metal has its own job.
fact: Strontium is a soft, silvery metal with the symbol Sr.
fact: The atomic number of strontium is 38.
fact: Strontium compounds give fireworks and flares a bright red colour.
fact: Strontium is named after Strontian, a village in Scotland where it was found.
fact: Strontium is in the same group of the periodic table as calcium and magnesium.
exercise: What colour does strontium give to fireworks? | Green | *Red | Yellow | Blue
exercise: Which symbol is used for strontium? | *Sr | St | Su | Sn
exercise: Strontium is named after a village in which country? | *Scotland | Ireland | England | Wales
test: Which metal gives fireworks a green colour? | Strontium | Sodium | *Barium | Copper
test: What kind of metal is strontium? | *Soft and silvery | Liquid and clear like water | Black and rough | Hard and heavy like iron
exam: What is the atomic number of strontium? | 29 | 20 | 11 | *38
